WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:05.703
Your brain has a great many named parts.

2
00:00:06.323 --> 00:00:07.923
Is one shape behind them all?

3
00:00:08.833 --> 00:00:13.873
Most neurons and most glia of your brain and spinal cord come from the wall of one

4
00:00:13.873 --> 00:00:16.753
tube. It forms along the embryo's back.

5
00:00:17.683 --> 00:00:22.483
The one idea: picture the central nervous system as one hollow tube, bent once.

6
00:00:23.517 --> 00:00:26.677
Its walls became the brain's parts and the spinal cord.

7
00:00:27.577 --> 00:00:29.197
Its canal became the ventricles.

8
00:00:30.117 --> 00:00:35.077
And the tube's own long axis, not the body's, sets what the direction words mean.

9
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:41.040
As it grows, the front end swells into three bulges: forebrain, midbrain and

10
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.040
hindbrain.

11
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:45.973
The forebrain and the hindbrain each divide in two.

12
00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:48.453
The rest becomes the spinal cord.

13
00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:54.147
The front of the forebrain buds out on each side, as the two cerebral hemispheres.

14
00:00:55.047 --> 00:00:57.387
Their canals become the two lateral ventricles.

15
00:00:58.317 --> 00:01:02.837
The back of the forebrain becomes the thalamus and hypothalamus, around the third

16
00:01:02.837 --> 00:01:03.837
ventricle.

17
00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:07.787
The midbrain keeps a narrow canal, the cerebral aqueduct.

18
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:12.920
The hindbrain becomes the pons and medulla, around the fourth ventricle.

19
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.800
The cerebellum grows from the hindbrain's dorsal side, so the fourth ventricle lies

20
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:19.800
beneath it.

21
00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:25.570
In the spinal cord, many neurons that receive sensory signals develop in the tube's

22
00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.610
dorsal half, and motor neurons in its ventral half.

23
00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:34.310
It is a plan, not a complete split: sensory fibres also reach ventral neurons.

24
00:01:35.233 --> 00:01:40.453
So sensory fibres enter through dorsal roots, and motor axons leave through ventral

25
00:01:40.453 --> 00:01:41.453
roots.

26
00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:45.867
The brainstem keeps this plan, with sensory nuclei moved to the side.

27
00:01:46.707 --> 00:01:48.107
The forebrain does not follow it.

28
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.180
In a rat, the tube runs nearly straight from nose to tail.

29
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.320
Dorsal is toward its back.

30
00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.610
Stand the rat up, face pointing forward.

31
00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:00.290
The body, cord and brainstem turn.

32
00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.690
Only the forebrain keeps its frame.

33
00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:08.670
In a human, the tube bends by roughly a right angle where the midbrain meets the

34
00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.950
diencephalon. The embryo bends in three places.

35
00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:14.050
This one matters for naming.

36
00:02:15.083 --> 00:02:19.463
You are standing. In your spinal cord, which way does dorsal point?

37
00:02:25.517 --> 00:02:29.097
Toward your back. The brainstem uses the same frame.

38
00:02:29.997 --> 00:02:32.577
In your forebrain, dorsal points to the top of your head.

39
00:02:33.517 --> 00:02:37.357
Three planes name the sections: sagittal, coronal and horizontal.

40
00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:42.647
A horizontal cut runs along the forebrain's axis.

41
00:02:43.627 --> 00:02:44.987
How does it pass through the brainstem?

42
00:02:51.017 --> 00:02:54.917
Roughly across it, because the brainstem's axis runs up and down.

43
00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.860
So the tube tells you where things are.

44
00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:00.400
What each place does is another question.
